==================================
If I wanted to demonstrate that I know enough about electrical circuits to provide technical support for phones and most household appliances, I would study practical electrical and electronic troubleshooting, not just electrical theory.

The goal would be to understand how electricity flows, how circuits work, how to read wiring diagrams, how to identify faulty components, and how to safely guide someone through diagnosing a problem.

I would organize the learning around three areas:

1. Electrical fundamentals: Voltage, current, resistance, power, AC and DC.
2. Electronics and circuit troubleshooting: Components, circuit boards, sensors, motors, relays, power supplies and control boards.
3. Appliance and phone systems: How common devices work, their common failures, and how to interpret their service diagrams.

One important distinction: phone repair is mostly low-voltage electronics, while household appliances can involve lethal mains voltage, high-current circuits, capacitors and other hazards. You should be able to diagnose and explain many problems without opening energized equipment or instructing an unqualified person to work on live circuits.

