## My final recommendation

If your goal is to become someone who can provide technical support for phones and most household appliances, I would aim for these three competency levels:

1. Electrical technician fundamentals: Understand circuits, components, power and safety.
2. Electronics troubleshooting: Understand circuit boards, sensors, motors, power supplies and measurement.
3. Appliance and phone technical support: Interpret service manuals, identify symptoms, explain likely faults and determine when professional repair is necessary.

I would also pursue an entry-level electronics or electrical fundamentals certificate, followed by appliance-specific training.

One question that would help me tailor the entire curriculum:

Are you trying to become:

- A. A remote technical support representative who helps customers troubleshoot appliances and phones over the phone.
- B. A hands-on electronics repair technician who physically diagnoses and repairs devices.
- C. A general appliance technical support specialist who understands refrigerators, washers, dryers, ovens, microwaves and other home equipment.
- D. All three — someone with broad electrical and electronics knowledge who can provide technical support across most consumer devices.

I would structure the depth of the training differently depending on which one you want.
